  "handoff": {
    "summary": "FFLAS dependency builds now publish one immutable, content-addressed GMP/Givaro/FFLAS-FFPACK/OpenBLAS prefix and materialize it in Unix worktrees as a read-only symlink. Installed config and pkg-config metadata is relocatable and path-bearing libtool archives are omitted. Fresh worktrees bootstrap only the TypeScript compiler plus Python/Sage parser assets; a content stamp and addon input identity reject stale compiler outputs. Publication uses process-birth ownership and a live helper heartbeat during synchronous builds. On bench-1, a fully empty clone completed FLINT plus FFLAS preparation in 5m25s; the hardened FFLAS generation built cold in 1m16s over the retained FLINT cache, and an identical warm prepare took 3.07s. After deleting the builder outputs, a detached worktree restored all four FLINT/FFLAS stages, traversed the relocated config prefix, and passed the FFLAS tests. Windows retains copy materialization.",
    "risks": [
      "Shared Unix materializations are absolute symlinks into the repository-common cache, so manually relocating only a worktree without its common Git directory breaks the link.",
      "Old content-addressed cache generations are retained indefinitely; a future concurrency-safe garbage collector should reclaim versions no live worktree or build references.",
      "Windows uses copied prefixes because developer-mode symlink capability is not guaranteed, so it receives build reuse but not the same disk deduplication."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integrate the lane and exercise the cache-hit smoke test on native Windows and macOS CI.",
      "Design conservative cache garbage collection after shared materialization has accumulated real-world usage."
    ]
  }
}
